%S A398818 1,2,3,5,6,7,8,10,11,13,14,15,17,19,21,22,23,24,26,27,29,30,31,33,34,
%T A398818 35,37,38,39,40,41,42,43,46,47,51,53,54,55,56,57,58,59,61,62,64,65,66,
%U A398818 67,69,70,71,73,74,77,78,79,82,83,85,86,87,88,89,91,93,94,95,97
%N A398818 Numbers whose powerful part (A057521) is a cube.
%C A398818 Numbers such that every exponent in their prime factorization is either 1 or a multiple of 3.
%C A398818 First differs from its subsequence A336591 at n = 46: a(46) = 64 = 2^6 is not a term in A336591.
%C A398818 First differs from A036537 at n = 46: a(46) = 64 is not a term in A036537. Also A036537(88) = 128 is the least term in A036537 that is not in this sequence.
%C A398818 First differs from A392631 at n = 46: a(46) = 64 is not a term in A392631. Also A392631(89) = 128 is the least term in A392631 that is not in this sequence.
%C A398818 All the squarefree numbers are terms since their powerful part is 1.
%C A398818 Each term in this sequence has a unique representation as the product of two coprime numbers: a squarefree number and a cube.
%C A398818 The asymptotic density of this sequence is zeta(3) * Product_{p prime} (1 - 1/p^2 - 1/p^4 + 1/p^5) = 0.692568372844624140241... .
%H A398818 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A398818/b398818.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A398818 <a href="/index/Eu#epf">Index entries for sequences computed from exponents in factorization of n</a>.
%e A398818 24 = 2^3 * 3 is a term since its powerful part, 8 = 2^3, is a cube.
%t A398818 q[k_] := AllTrue[FactorInteger[k][[;;, 2]], # == 1 || Divisible[#, 3] &]; Select[Range[100], q]
%o A398818 (PARI) isok(k) = #select(x -> (x > 1 && x%3), factor(k)[,2]) == 0;
%Y A398818 Subsequences: A000578, A005117, A336591, A398819.
%Y A398818 Cf. A002117, A008486, A057521, A335275 (numbers whose powerful part is a square), A392631.
%K A398818 nonn,easy,new
%O A398818 1,2
%A A398818 _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 11 2026
